/* ---------------- question sets ---------------- */
const SETS = [
 {id:"h1", lv:1, title:"Heat basics", zh:"看图：热的基础", qs:[
  {pic:["labthermo"], q:"What is this instrument used for?", o:["measuring how hot or cold something is","measuring how heavy something is","measuring how long something is","measuring how much space something takes up"], a:0,
   e:"A thermometer measures temperature — how hot or cold something is — in degrees Celsius (°C).", z:"温度计用来量温度。"},
  {q:"Temperature is measured in ___.", o:["degrees Celsius (°C)","cubic centimetres (cm³)","kilograms (kg)","millilitres (ml)"], a:0,
   e:"Temperature is measured in degrees Celsius (°C) with a thermometer. cm³ and ml are units of volume; kg is a unit of mass.", z:"温度的单位是 °C。"},
  {pic:["bridgejoint"], q:"Gaps like this are left in bridges. Why?", o:["To give the bridge space to expand on hot days.","To let rainwater drain away.","To save building materials.","To give the bridge space to contract on hot days."], a:0,
   e:"On hot days the bridge gains heat and expands. The gap gives it space to expand without bending or cracking.", z:"桥受热会膨胀，留缝给它空间。"},
  {pic:["vacuumflask"], q:"A vacuum flask keeps soup hot for hours because it ___.", o:["slows down heat loss from the soup","gives out heat to keep the soup warm","keeps the cold air away from the soup","lets heat flow out of the soup quickly"], a:0,
   e:"A flask does not make heat. It slows down the heat flowing out of the soup. Cold does not flow.", z:"保温瓶减慢热的流失，它本身不会产生热。"},
  {pic:["ovenglove"], q:"Oven gloves are made of thick cloth because cloth is ___.", o:["a poor conductor of heat","a good conductor of heat","a light source","waterproof"], a:0,
   e:"Cloth is a poor conductor of heat, so heat from a hot tray flows to the hand slowly.", z:"布是热的不良导体。"},
  {pic:["woodspoon"], q:"Why is a wooden spoon good for stirring hot soup?", o:["Wood is a poor conductor of heat, so the handle heats up slowly.","Wood is a good conductor of heat, so the soup cooks more quickly.","Wood soaks up the heat from the soup, so the soup cools down.","Wood gives out cold, so the handle stays cool enough to hold."], a:0,
   e:"Heat flows through wood slowly, so the handle stays cool enough to hold. Wood does not give out cold — cold does not flow.", z:"木头是热的不良导体，手柄不容易变烫。"},
  {pic:["woolsweater"], q:"A wool sweater keeps us warm on a cold day because it ___.", o:["traps air and slows down heat loss from our body","gives out its own heat to warm our body","is a good conductor of heat, so it warms us","stops the cold air from flowing into our body"], a:0,
   e:"Wool traps air, which is a poor conductor of heat. This slows down the heat flowing from our body to the cold air.", z:"羊毛衣困住空气，减慢身体散热。"},
  {q:"Which of these is a good conductor of heat?", o:["copper","wood","plastic","rubber"], a:0,
   e:"Copper is a metal. Metals are good conductors of heat.", z:"铜是金属，是热的良导体。"},
  {q:"Which of these is a poor conductor of heat?", o:["styrofoam","aluminium","steel","iron"], a:0,
   e:"Aluminium, steel and iron are metals — good conductors. Styrofoam is a poor conductor.", z:"泡沫塑料是热的不良导体。"},
  {pic:["coolerbox"], q:"Drinks and ice are kept in a cooler box at a picnic. Why does the ice melt slowly?", o:["The box is made of a poor conductor of heat, so heat flows in slowly.","The box gives out cold air, which keeps the ice frozen for longer.","The box is made of a good conductor of heat, so the cold stays in.","The box makes the ice colder, so it takes much longer to melt."], a:0,
   e:"The walls of a cooler box slow down the heat flowing from the warm air to the ice. The box does not give out cold.", z:"保冷箱减慢热流进箱子。"}
 ]},
 {id:"h2", lv:2, title:"Heat flow & temperature", zh:"热的流动和温度", qs:[
  {q:"Which statement is correct?", o:["Heat is a form of energy. Temperature measures how hot or cold something is.", "Heat is a form of energy. Heat also measures how hot or cold something is.", "Temperature is a form of energy. It measures how hot or cold something is.", "Temperature is a form of energy. Heat measures how hot or cold something is."], a:0,
   e:"Heat is a form of energy. Temperature is a measure of how hot or cold something is, and it is the temperature that we measure in °C. Temperature is not energy.", z:"热是能量；温度表示冷热程度，用 °C 表示。"},
  {q:"A cup of hot tea is left on a table. What happens to it?", o:["It loses heat to the cooler surroundings until it reaches room temperature.", "It loses heat to the cooler surroundings until it is colder than the room.", "It gains cold from the cooler surroundings until it reaches room temperature.", "It gains cold from the cooler surroundings until it is colder than the room."], a:0,
   e:"Heat flows from the hotter tea to the cooler air until both are at the same temperature, so it stops at room temperature. Cold does not flow.", z:"热茶散热，降到室温为止。"},
  {q:"A can of cold drink is taken out of the fridge and left on the table. Which is correct?", o:["It gains heat from the warmer air around it until it reaches room temperature.", "It gains heat from the warmer air around it until it becomes warmer than the room.", "It loses its cold to the warmer air around it until it reaches room temperature.", "It loses its cold to the warmer air around it until it becomes warmer than the room."], a:0,
   e:"The air around the can is warmer, so heat flows into the can until it reaches room temperature, and then it stops. Cold does not flow.", z:"冷饮从周围吸热，温度上升。"},
  {q:"Mei holds an ice cube. Why does her hand feel cold?", o:["Heat flows from her hand to the ice cube.","Cold flows from the ice cube to her hand.","The ice cube gives out heat to her hand.","Her hand gains heat from the ice cube."], a:0,
   e:"Her hand is hotter than the ice, so heat flows from her hand to the ice. Her hand loses heat and feels cold.", z:"热从手流向冰块，手失去热所以觉得冷。"},
  {ch:"ht_tsame", q:"Which beaker has MORE heat, P or Q?", o:["Q — it has more water at the same temperature.","P — it has less water.","Both have the same heat, because they are at the same temperature.","P — it is hotter."], a:0,
   e:"P and Q are at the same temperature, but Q has three times as much water, so Q has more heat.", z:"温度一样，水越多，热越多。"},
  {ch:"ht_tsame", q:"Which beaker has MORE heat, P or R?", o:["R — it has the same amount of water at a higher temperature.","P — it is cooler.","Both have the same heat, because they have the same amount of water.","Both have the same heat, because both are hot."], a:0,
   e:"P and R have the same amount of water, but R is hotter, so R has more heat.", z:"水量一样，温度越高，热越多。"},
  {q:"When the liquid in a thermometer moves UP, the liquid has ___.", o:["gained heat and expanded","lost heat and contracted","gained heat and contracted","lost heat and expanded"], a:0,
   e:"The liquid gains heat, expands and needs more space, so it moves up the thin tube.", z:"液体受热膨胀，往上升。"},
  {q:"Which is most likely the body temperature of a healthy person?", o:["37 °C","27 °C","47 °C","100 °C"], a:0,
   e:"Normal body temperature is about 37 °C. 100 °C is the boiling point of pure water.", z:"正常体温约 37°C。"},
  {q:"Ice cubes are put into a glass of warm water. Which statements are correct?", st:["The water loses heat to the ice.","The ice gains heat and melts.","Cold flows from the ice into the water.","The temperature of the water falls."], o:["A and B only","A, B and D only","B, C and D only","A, B, C and D"], a:1,
   e:"Heat flows from the warm water to the ice. Cold does not flow.", z:"热从水流向冰；冷不会流动。"},
  {q:"A metal block at 80 °C is placed touching a metal block at 20 °C. After some time, ___.", o:["both blocks are at the same temperature", "the hot block becomes colder than the other block", "the cold block stays at 20 °C", "the cold block warms up to 80 °C"], a:0,
   e:"Heat flows from the hotter block to the colder block until both are at the same temperature (somewhere between 20 °C and 80 °C).", z:"热从热的流向冷的，直到温度相同。"}
 ]},
 {id:"h3", lv:2, title:"Expansion & conductors", zh:"热胀冷缩和导体", qs:[
  {q:"Why are small gaps left between sections of railway track?", o:["To give the tracks space to expand on hot days.","To give the tracks space to contract on hot days.","To make the train ride quieter.","To let rainwater drain away."], a:0,
   e:"On hot days the steel tracks gain heat and expand. Without gaps, they could bend.", z:"铁轨受热膨胀，要留缝。"},
  {pic:["pingpong"], q:"A ping-pong ball has a dent. It is put into hot water, and the dent pops out. Why?", o:["The air inside the ball gains heat and expands.","The air inside the ball loses heat and contracts.","Hot water gets into the ball and pushes the dent out.","The plastic shell becomes smaller."], a:0,
   e:"The air inside gains heat from the hot water and expands, pushing the dent out.", z:"球里的空气受热膨胀，把凹处顶出来。"},
  {q:"Kai cannot open a tight metal lid on a glass jar. What should he do?", o:["Run hot water over the metal lid.","Put the jar in the freezer.","Run cold water over the lid.","Leave the jar in a dark cupboard."], a:0,
   e:"The metal lid gains heat and expands, so it becomes looser. Cooling would make it contract and become tighter.", z:"热水冲金属盖子，盖子膨胀变松。"},
  {ch:"ht_balloon", q:"What happens to the balloon after a few minutes?", o:["It becomes bigger, because the air in the flask gains heat and expands.","It becomes smaller, because the air in the flask loses heat and contracts.","It does not change, because air cannot expand.","It becomes bigger, because hot water goes into the balloon."], a:0,
   e:"The air in the flask gains heat from the hot water and expands into the balloon.", z:"瓶里的空气受热膨胀，气球变大。"},
  {pic:["fryingpan"], q:"Why is the pan part of a frying pan made of metal?", o:["Metal is a good conductor of heat, so heat flows quickly to the food.","Metal is a poor conductor of heat, so the food does not get burnt.","Metal gives out its own heat, so the food cooks faster.","Metal is shiny, so it reflects the heat onto the food."], a:0,
   e:"Metals are good conductors of heat, so heat flows quickly from the fire to the food and it cooks quickly.", z:"金属是热的良导体，热很快传到食物。"},
  {q:"In which group are ALL the materials poor conductors of heat?", o:["wood, plastic, wool","wood, copper, wool","steel, rubber, cloth","aluminium, iron, glass"], a:0,
   e:"Copper, steel, aluminium and iron are metals — good conductors of heat.", z:"铜、钢、铝、铁都是热的良导体。"},
  {q:"A metal spoon and a wooden spoon have been on the same table for hours. The metal spoon feels colder. Why?", o:["Metal conducts heat away from the hand faster than wood does.","The metal spoon is at a lower temperature than the wooden one.","Metal gives out cold, which flows into the hand quickly.","Wood gives out heat, so the wooden spoon feels warmer."], a:0,
   e:"Both spoons are at room temperature. Metal is a good conductor, so heat flows from the hand into the metal spoon faster, and the hand feels colder.", z:"两把勺温度一样；金属把手上的热传走得快。"},
  {q:"Ali wrapped one ice cube in a thick cloth and left another ice cube unwrapped. Which is correct?", o:["The wrapped ice cube melts more slowly, as the cloth slows down heat gain.","The wrapped ice cube melts faster, as the thick cloth keeps it warm.","Both melt at the same rate, as cloth does not affect heat flow.","The wrapped ice cube never melts, as the cloth stops all heat flowing in."], a:0,
   e:"Cloth is a poor conductor of heat. It slows down the heat flowing from the warm surroundings into the ice, but it does not stop it. Cloth does not make things warm.", z:"布减慢冰块从周围吸热，冰融化得慢。"},
  {q:"Which statement about clothes is correct?", o:["Clothes slow down heat loss from our body.", "Clothes take in heat from the air and pass it to us.", "Thick clothes produce heat to keep us warm.", "A jacket stops the cold from flowing into the skin."], a:0,
   e:"Our body produces heat. Clothes only slow down the loss of that heat. They do not make heat, and cold does not flow.", z:"衣服不产生热，只减慢身体散热。"},
  {q:"On a hot, sunny day, a metal slide and a plastic slide are at about the same temperature. The metal slide feels much hotter. Why?", o:["Metal conducts heat to our hand faster than plastic does.", "Metal gives out its own heat when the sun shines on it.", "Plastic slides reflect sunlight, so they always stay cool.", "The metal slide is much hotter than the plastic slide."], a:0,
   e:"Both slides are at about the same temperature, so neither stays cool. Metal is a good conductor of heat, so heat flows from the slide into our hand faster.", z:"金属导热快，热很快传到手上。"}
 ]},
 {id:"h4", lv:3, title:"Experiments & data", zh:"实验和数据", qs:[
  {ch:"ht_thermoQ", q:"What temperature does this thermometer show?", o:["32 °C","34 °C","36 °C","43 °C"], a:1, fix:true,
   e:"Between 30 °C and 40 °C there are 5 small spaces, so each small space is 2 °C. The liquid is 2 spaces above 30 °C: 34 °C.", z:"每小格 2°C，30 往上两格 = 34°C。"},
  {ch:"ht_rods", q:"To make this a fair test, which must be kept the same?", st:["the material of the rods","the length and thickness of the rods","the amount of wax on each rod","the temperature of the hot water"], o:["A and B only","B, C and D only","A, C and D only","A, B, C and D"], a:1,
   e:"The material is what is being tested, so it is the only thing that changes.", z:"材料是要测试的，只有它不同。"},
  {ch:"ht_twax", q:"Which rod is the BEST conductor of heat?", o:["W","X","Y","Z"], a:1, fix:true,
   e:"Heat flowed up rod X fastest, so its wax melted first (2 min).", z:"蜡最快融化 → 最好的导体。"},
  {ch:"ht_twax", q:"Which rod is most likely made of wood?", o:["W","X","Y","Z"], a:3, fix:true,
   e:"Wood is a poor conductor of heat. Very little heat flowed up rod Z, so its wax did not melt.", z:"木头导热差，蜡不融化 → Z。"},
  {ch:"ht_twax", q:"Arrange the rods from the BEST to the POOREST conductor of heat.", o:["X, Y, W, Z","Z, W, Y, X","X, W, Y, Z","W, X, Y, Z"], a:0, fix:true,
   e:"The shorter the time taken for the wax to melt, the better the conductor: X (2 min), Y (5 min), W (9 min), Z (did not melt).", z:"时间越短，导热越好。"},
  {ch:"ht_beakers", q:"To make this a fair test, which must be kept the same?", st:["the amount of hot water in each beaker","the wrapping material","the starting temperature of the water","the thickness of the wrapping"], o:["A and C only","A, C and D only","B, C and D only","A, B, C and D"], a:1,
   e:"Only the wrapping material is changed. The amount and starting temperature of the water, and the thickness of the wrapping, stay the same.", z:"只改变包裹材料。"},
  {ch:"ht_tcool", q:"Which wrapping material is the POOREST conductor of heat?", o:["P","Q","R"], a:1, fix:true,
   e:"The water wrapped in Q lost heat the most slowly (still 66 °C after 20 min), so Q is the poorest conductor.", z:"水降温最慢 → 最差的导体 → Q。"},
  {ch:"ht_bice", q:"Which material is the most suitable for making a cooler box?", o:["W","X","Y","Z"], a:1, fix:true,
   e:"The ice wrapped in X took the longest to melt, so X slowed down heat gain from the surroundings the most.", z:"冰融化最慢 → 吸热最慢 → X。"},
  {ch:"ht_tmix", q:"Which statement is correct?", o:["Heat flowed from the water in the beaker to the water in the basin until both were at 44 °C.","Cold flowed from the water in the basin into the water in the beaker until both were at 44 °C.","The water in the basin lost heat to the water in the beaker until both were at 44 °C.","The water in the beaker gained heat from the water in the basin until both were at 44 °C."], a:0,
   e:"The water in the beaker was hotter, so it lost heat to the water in the basin. At 8 min both were at 44 °C. Cold does not flow.", z:"热从烧杯里的热水流向盆里的水，直到温度相同。"},
  {ch:"ht_trodfair", q:"Jun wants to find out if the MATERIAL of a rod affects how fast heat flows through it. Which two rods should he use?", o:["K and L","K and M","K and N","L and N"], a:0,
   e:"K and L have the same length and thickness; only the material is different. K and M differ in length; N is thicker as well as a different material.", z:"只能有材料不同：K 和 L。"}
 ]},
 {id:"h5", lv:3, title:"AL1 challenge", zh:"难题挑战", qs:[
  {ch:"ht_pot", q:"Which parts of the pot should be made of a POOR conductor of heat?", o:["X and Z only","Y only","X and Y only","X, Y and Z"], a:0,
   e:"The handle (X) and the lid knob (Z) are held by hand, so heat should flow to them slowly. The base (Y) should be a good conductor so that heat flows quickly to the food.", z:"手柄和盖钮用不良导体，锅底用良导体。"},
  {ch:"ht_bcups", q:"Which cup is most likely made of styrofoam?", o:["Cup J","Cup K","Cup L","Cup M"], a:1, fix:true,
   e:"Styrofoam is a poor conductor of heat, so the water in it lost heat the most slowly and stayed the hottest (62 °C).", z:"泡沫塑料导热最差，水最热 → K。"},
  {q:"Which statements are correct?", st:["A good conductor of heat is always the best material to use.","Air is a poor conductor of heat.","Metals are good conductors of heat.","Clothes produce heat to keep us warm."], o:["A and B only","B and C only","B, C and D only","A, B and C only"], a:1,
   e:"A pot handle should be a poor conductor, so A is wrong. Clothes only slow down heat loss, so D is wrong.", z:"良导体不一定最好用；衣服不产生热。"},
  {q:"Ken put a metal spoon at room temperature (30 °C) into a bowl of soup at 70 °C. Which is correct?", o:["Heat flows from the soup to the spoon, so the spoon becomes warmer.","Cold flows from the spoon into the soup, so the soup becomes cooler.","Heat flows from the spoon to the soup, so the soup becomes warmer.","The spoon and the soup do not affect each other, so both stay the same."], a:0,
   e:"Heat flows from the hotter soup to the cooler spoon. The spoon gains heat and the soup loses a little heat.", z:"热从汤流向汤匙。"},
  {ch:"ht_tmix", q:"If the readings were taken for two more hours, what would the temperatures most likely be?", o:["Both about 30 °C","Both still 44 °C","Beaker about 44 °C, basin about 30 °C","Both about 0 °C"], a:0,
   e:"At 44 °C both are still warmer than the room (30 °C), so both keep losing heat to the surroundings until they reach room temperature.", z:"两者都比室温高，会继续散热，直到降到室温。"},
  {q:"Wearing three thin shirts can keep you warmer than wearing one thick shirt. Why?", o:["The layers trap more air, and air is a poor conductor of heat.","Three thin shirts give out more heat than one thick shirt.","Thin shirts are better conductors of heat, so they warm you up.","The layers stop the cold air outside from flowing into the body."], a:0,
   e:"Air trapped between the layers slows down heat loss from the body. Shirts do not give out heat, and cold does not flow.", z:"多层衣服之间困住更多空气。"},
  {q:"Which of these shows that GAINING heat can change the state of a material?", o:["Butter melts in a hot pan.","Water in an ice tray freezes in the freezer.","A balloon becomes smaller in the fridge.","A metal rod becomes slightly longer when heated."], a:0,
   e:"Melting (solid → liquid) is caused by gaining heat. Freezing is caused by losing heat. The balloon and the rod only change size, not state.", z:"融化是吸热引起的状态变化。"},
  {ch:"ht_balloon", q:"After the balloon has become bigger, the flask is moved into a basin of ice water. What happens to the balloon?", o:["It becomes smaller, because the air inside loses heat and contracts.","It becomes bigger, because the air inside gains cold and expands.","It becomes bigger, because cold air takes up more space.","It does not change, because air cannot contract once it has expanded."], a:0,
   e:"The air in the flask loses heat to the ice water and contracts, so the balloon becomes smaller.", z:"空气散热收缩，气球变小。"},
  {q:"A pot has a copper base and a plastic handle. Which statements are correct?", st:["Copper is a good conductor of heat, so heat flows quickly to the food.","Plastic is a poor conductor of heat, so the handle heats up slowly.","Copper is used because it is a poor conductor of heat."], o:["A only","A and B only","B and C only","A, B and C"], a:1,
   e:"Copper is a good conductor, not a poor one.", z:"铜是热的良导体。"},
  {pic:["vacuumflask"], q:"Can a vacuum flask also stop ice cubes from melting quickly?", o:["Yes — it slows down heat flowing into the flask from outside.","No — a flask can only slow down heat loss, not heat gain.","Yes — it gives out cold to the ice, which keeps it frozen.","No — its metal walls let heat flow in quickly from outside."], a:0,
   e:"A flask slows down heat flow both ways: out of hot drinks and into cold ones.", z:"保温瓶两个方向都减慢热的流动。"}
 ]},
 {id:"h6", lv:4, title:"Full-mark answers", zh:"选出能拿满分的答案", qs:[
  {q:"A metal spoon and a wooden spoon are in the same room. Explain why the metal spoon feels colder. [2 marks]\nWhich answer gets full marks?", o:["Metal is a good conductor of heat, so heat flows from my hand to it faster than to wood.", "Metal is a good conductor of heat, so cold flows from it into my hand faster than from wood.", "The metal spoon is at a lower temperature, so heat flows from my hand to it faster than to wood.", "The metal spoon is at a lower temperature, so cold flows from it into my hand faster than from wood."], a:0,
   n:["✓ Property (good conductor) + direction of heat flow (from the hand to the metal, faster) — 2 marks.", "½ The property is right, but cold does not flow. Heat flows FROM the hand TO the metal.", "½ The heat flow is right, but both spoons are at the SAME (room) temperature. The reason is that metal is a good conductor.", "✗ Both spoons are at the same temperature, and cold does not flow."],
   e:"Give the property AND the direction of heat flow: heat flows from the hand to the metal faster.", z:"写性质，也写热从手流到金属，而且流得更快。"},
  {q:"Explain why the liquid in a thermometer moves up when the thermometer is put into hot water. [1 mark]", o:["The liquid gains heat from the hot water and expands, so it moves up the tube.","The liquid gets hot from the hot water and becomes bigger, so it moves up the tube.","The hot water presses on the bulb and squeezes the liquid up the thin tube.","The liquid gains heat from the hot water and rises, because hot liquids are lighter."], a:0,
   n:["✓ Gains heat + expands.","½ Everyday words: write 'gains heat' and 'expands', not 'gets hot' and 'becomes bigger'.","✗ The liquid is not squeezed up. It gains heat and expands.","½ 'Gains heat' is right, but the liquid moves up because it EXPANDS, not because hot liquid rises."],
   e:"Link the heat gain to the expansion: gains heat → expands → moves up the tube.", z:"吸热 → 膨胀 → 上升。"},
  {q:"Explain why gaps are left between sections of railway track. [2 marks]", o:["On hot days the steel tracks gain heat and expand. The gaps give them space to expand without bending.","On hot days the steel tracks gain heat and contract. The gaps give them space so that they do not bend.","On hot days the steel tracks get hot and grow longer. The gaps stop them from bending out of shape.","The gaps let air flow under the steel tracks, so that the tracks stay cool and do not bend on hot days."], a:0,
   n:["✓ Gains heat + expands, and why the gap helps (space to expand) — 2 marks.","½ The gap part is right, but things that gain heat EXPAND — they do not contract.","½ Everyday words: write 'gain heat' and 'expand', not 'get hot' and 'grow longer'.","✗ Not the reason. The gaps give the tracks space to EXPAND."],
   e:"Say what happens (gain heat, expand) and why the gap is needed (space to expand).", z:"写受热膨胀，也写缝的作用：给铁轨膨胀的空间。"},
  {ch:"ht_balloon", q:"Explain why the balloon became bigger. [2 marks]", o:["The air in the flask gained heat from the hot water and expanded into the balloon.", "The air in the flask gained heat from the hot water and rose into the balloon, as hot air rises.", "The air in the flask got hot from the hot water and expanded into the balloon.", "The air in the flask got hot from the hot water and rose into the balloon, as hot air rises."], a:0,
   n:["✓ Both keywords: GAINED HEAT (from the hot water) + EXPANDED — 2 marks.", "½ 'Gained heat' is right, but the balloon grows because the air EXPANDS, not because hot air rises.", "½ 'Expanded' is right, but 'got hot' is everyday language. Write 'gained heat from the hot water'.", "✗ Everyday words and the wrong reason: write 'gained heat' and 'expanded'."],
   e:"Use \"gained heat\" and \"expanded\".", z:"关键词：吸热、膨胀。"},
  {pic:["woolsweater"], q:"Explain how a wool sweater keeps a person warm on a cold day. [2 marks]", o:["Wool traps air, a poor conductor of heat, so it slows down heat loss from the body to the cold air.", "Wool traps air, a poor conductor of heat, so it stops the cold air from getting into the body.", "Wool is thick, soft and warm, so it slows down heat loss from the body to the cold air.", "Wool is thick, soft and warm, so it stops the cold air from getting into the body."], a:0,
   n:["✓ Traps air (a poor conductor of heat) + slows down heat LOSS from the body — 2 marks.", "½ The first part is right, but cold does not flow. Say it slows down heat LOSS from the body.", "½ The heat-loss part is right, but 'thick, soft and warm' is not the reason. Say it traps air, a POOR CONDUCTOR of heat.", "✗ Everyday words, and cold does not flow. Wool traps air and slows down heat loss."],
   e:"Say why (traps air, a poor conductor) and what slows down (heat loss from the body to the cold air).", z:"写清楚：困住空气（热的不良导体）→ 减慢身体的热散失到冷空气中。"},
  {q:"Explain why ice cubes in a glass of water melt. [1 mark]", o:["The ice cubes gain heat from the warmer water, so they melt.","The ice cubes give out cold to the water, so they melt.","The ice cubes lose heat to the warmer water, so they melt.","The water is warmer than the ice cubes, so the ice cubes melt."], a:0,
   n:["✓ Gains heat from the warmer water.","✗ Cold does not flow. Only heat flows.","✗ Reversed: heat flows FROM the warmer water TO the ice.","✗ True, but it does not say that the ice GAINS HEAT from the water."],
   e:"Melting needs heat. Say where the heat comes from: the ice gains heat from the warmer water.", z:"融化需要吸热，写热从哪里来。"},
  {ch:"ht_tcool", q:"Which material should be used to make a flask to keep soup hot? Explain. [2 marks]", o:["Q. Its water lost heat the most slowly (66 °C at 20 min), so Q slows down heat loss from the soup.","Q. It is the best conductor of heat, so it will keep the heat inside the soup for much longer.","R. Its water lost heat the fastest (42 °C at 20 min), so R lets the least heat escape from the soup.","Q. Its water was still at 66 °C after 20 minutes, the highest reading of all three beakers."], a:0,
   n:["✓ Choice + evidence from the table + reason (slows down heat loss) — 2 marks.","½ Q is right, but Q is the POOREST conductor of heat. A good conductor would let the soup lose heat quickly.","✗ The water that lost heat fastest was wrapped in the BEST conductor — the worst choice for a flask.","½ Q and the data are right, but there is no reason: say that Q slows down heat loss from the soup."],
   e:"Use the data as evidence, then give the reason.", z:"用数据做证据，再解释。"},
  {ch:"ht_tcool", q:"Why must the same amount of water at the same starting temperature be used in each beaker? [1 mark]", o:["To make it a fair test, so that only the wrapping material affects how fast the water cools.","To make it a fair test, so that the results of the whole experiment will be accurate.","So that the water in all three beakers will cool down at exactly the same rate.","So that the wrapping material and its thickness are the same for every one of the beakers."], a:0,
   n:["✓ Fair test + names the one thing being tested (the wrapping material).","½ 'Fair test' is right, but fair for what? Name the one thing being tested.","✗ If the water cooled at the same rate in every beaker, there would be nothing to compare.","✗ The wrapping material is the one thing that is CHANGED (only its thickness stays the same)."],
   e:"Name the one thing that is being changed: only the wrapping material.", z:"写出只改变的那个条件：包裹材料。"},
  {pic:["coolerbox"], q:"A pupil wrote, \"A cooler box keeps the cold in.\" Which answer explains it correctly? [1 mark]", o:["The box is made of a poor conductor of heat, so it slows down heat gain from the warm air outside.","The box is made of a poor conductor of heat, so it slows down the cold escaping to the air outside.","The box is made of a poor conductor of heat, so heat flows through its walls more slowly.","The box is made of a good conductor of heat, so the heat in the box escapes to the outside quickly."], a:0,
   n:["✓ Poor conductor + slows down heat GAIN from the warm surroundings.","✗ Cold does not flow. The box slows down HEAT flowing IN.","½ Which way does the heat flow? Say it slows down heat GAIN from outside.","✗ A good conductor would let heat flow IN quickly, and the ice would melt fast."],
   e:"Talk about heat, never about cold flowing: the box slows down heat gain from the surroundings.", z:"只写热的流动，不写冷的流动：减慢从外面吸热。"},
  {pic:["fryingpan"], q:"Explain why a pan is made of metal but its handle is made of plastic. [2 marks]", o:["Metal is a good conductor of heat, so heat flows quickly to the food. Plastic is a poor conductor, so heat flows slowly to the hand.","Metal is a good conductor of heat, so the food cooks quickly. Plastic is a poor conductor, so the handle never gets hot at all.","Metal is a good conductor of heat and plastic is a poor conductor of heat, so they are used for the pan and the handle.","Metal is a poor conductor of heat, so it keeps the food warm. Plastic is a good conductor, so the handle stays cool."], a:0,
   n:["✓ Both parts, each with the property AND its effect on the heat — 2 marks.","½ The pan part is right, but 'never gets hot at all' is an over-statement: heat flows through plastic SLOWLY.","½ Properties only — say what each one does to the heat flow.","✗ The properties are swapped: metal is a GOOD conductor and plastic is a POOR conductor."],
   e:"Two parts → two explanations, each with its effect.", z:"两部分都要写性质和作用。"}
 ]}
];

